Prefill ALSA duplex playback so each block lands ahead of the hardware.

JUCE 8.0.14's ALSAThread links the capture and playback streams, prepares
both, and starts them with the first capture read. The playback buffer is
empty at that moment, and since the loop reads a block, processes it and only
then writes it, every write lands exactly where the hardware is already
reading. Stop threshold is the boundary, so ALSA never reports that as an
xrun: the stream simply stays on the edge for the life of the device.

On a USB interface with implicit feedback (Focusrite Scarlett 3rd gen,
measured) the next playback packet is queued at the very moment the capture
packet wakes the audio thread, so the block always loses that race: steady
crackle from the first launch, cured only by a reopen (changing the sample
rate and back) that happens to start on a better phase. Measured through
/proc/asound on the running app: playback fill -4..0 frames at launch.

Write two periods of silence into the playback stream before the thread
starts. Playback then runs a full period ahead (fill 124..254 frames on the
same Scarlett at 128/48k, holding across a 50 ms stall), which is exactly the
budget the callback needs to process a block before the hardware wants it.
One period is not enough: it puts the write back on the same race. Duplex
only - an output-only loop is paced by the playback stream and fills its own
buffer.

diff --git a/modules/juce_audio_devices/native/juce_ALSA_linux.cpp b/modules/juce_audio_devices/native/juce_ALSA_linux.cpp
--- a/modules/juce_audio_devices/native/juce_ALSA_linux.cpp
+++ b/modules/juce_audio_devices/native/juce_ALSA_linux.cpp
@@ -630,6 +630,24 @@ public:
         if (outputDevice != nullptr && JUCE_ALSA_FAILED (snd_pcm_prepare (outputDevice->handle)))
             return;
 
+        // PLECTRIFY_ALSA_DUPLEX_PREFILL: a duplex loop is paced by the capture
+        // stream, and the linked playback stream starts with nothing in it, so
+        // each block is written exactly where the hardware is already reading.
+        // On a USB interface with implicit feedback the next playback packet is
+        // queued at the very moment the capture packet wakes this thread, so
+        // the block always arrives too late for it: steady crackle, fixed only
+        // by a reopen that happens to land on a better phase. Two periods of
+        // silence put playback a full period ahead, which is the budget the
+        // callback needs to process a block before the hardware wants it.
+        if (inputDevice != nullptr && outputDevice != nullptr)
+        {
+            outputChannelBuffer.clear();
+
+            for (int i = 0; i < 2; ++i)
+                if (! outputDevice->writeToOutputDevice (outputChannelBuffer, bufferSize))
+                    break;
+        }
+
         startThread (Priority::high);
 
         int count = 1000;
